<h1>Front-end and UI/UX work — shown, not just described</h1>
<p class="lead">The DOM DoIT job description asks for a portfolio that shows UI work, not only backend code. This page is that evidence: working components, interaction patterns, and accessibility fixes, each tied to a case study in <code>docs/frontend-uiux-case-studies.md</code> and to the author's Iowa-tailored resume. No Figma files are published here — the Figma work in those cases came from client UX teams — but the translation of a design specification into a working, accessible interface is what this page demonstrates.</p>

<h2>How I work from a design</h2>
<ol>
    <li><strong>Read the specification:</strong> spacing, type, breakpoints, interaction behaviour, and validation states — not just the picture.</li>
    <li><strong>Tokens first:</strong> colour, spacing, and type from design tokens, so a second team can adopt the components without copying pages.</li>
    <li><strong>Semantic structure before styling:</strong> landmarks, heading order, labelled controls, plain-language errors.</li>
    <li><strong>Every state, designed:</strong> default, focus, error, success, disabled — for every control, plus what happens when a step fails.</li>
    <li><strong>Keyboard and screen reader before sign-off:</strong> keyboard-only navigation, focus management and return, JAWS/NVDA, axe remediation, then a regression pass each release.</li>
    <li><strong>Demo to UX and product before release:</strong> walk through interaction, validation, and mobile layouts, and close gaps before release.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Case studies</h2>
<div class="card-grid">
    <div class="card"><h3>Centene — Member Self-Service Portal Modernization</h3><p>Figma → responsive semantic HTML5/Sass/JavaScript. Enterprise design system + Storybook, Bootstrap screens migrated to design-system components, axe-core in Azure DevOps. Accessibility fixes in claims filters, date pickers, modal dialogs, and multi-step forms, checked with JAWS, NVDA, and axe.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Frontiers — Author and Reviewer Submission Platform</h3><p>Shared component library: form fields, tables, and alerts with documented states, accessibility notes, and do/don't guidance. Multi-step submission simplified with progress steps, inline validation, and save-and-resume. WCAG fixes via axe/WAVE, checked with NVDA/JAWS.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>New Jersey DOL — Unemployment Insurance Claimant Services</h3><p>Mobile-friendly weekly certification, layout tables replaced with semantic markup, plain-language questions and error messages, shared UI controls to state standards. Tested with JAWS, keyboard-only navigation, and axe before each release.</p></div>
</div>
<p>Full detail, with what to look at in code for each case, is in <code>docs/frontend-uiux-case-studies.md</code>.</p>

<h2>Interactive demo 1 — multi-step form with progress, inline validation, and save-and-resume</h2>
<p>This is the Frontiers submission pattern, in miniature. It works without JavaScript too — the server Apply page is the no-JS version — but with JavaScript it shows progress, inline errors, and a saved draft note. Try it: leave a field empty, then try a bad email.</p>
<p id="ux-form-status" role="status"></p>
<div id="ux-error-summary" class="error-summary" hidden><h2>There is a problem</h2><ul id="ux-error-list"></ul></div>
<p><strong>Step <span id="ux-step-label">1 of 3</span>:</strong> <span id="ux-step-name">Your details</span></p>
<form id="ux-form" novalidate>
    <div id="ux-step-1">
        <div class="form-group"><label class="form-label" for="UxFirstName">First name</label><input class="form-control" id="UxFirstName" autocomplete="given-name"></div>
        <div class="form-group"><label class="form-label" for="UxLastName">Last name</label><input class="form-control" id="UxLastName" autocomplete="family-name"></div>
    </div>
    <div id="ux-step-2" hidden>
        <div class="form-group"><label class="form-label" for="UxEmail">Email address</label><span class="form-hint" id="ux-email-hint">For example, jordan@example.com</span><input class="form-control" id="UxEmail" type="email" aria-describedby="ux-email-hint" autocomplete="email"></div>
        <div class="form-group"><label class="form-label" for="UxCounty">County</label><input class="form-control" id="UxCounty" value="Polk"></div>
    </div>
    <div id="ux-step-3" hidden>
        <div class="form-group"><input type="checkbox" id="UxTerms"> <label for="UxTerms">I understand this is a fictional sample application.</label></div>
        <p>Review: check your details above with the Back button before submitting. In a real submission form, this step would show a read-only summary — the pattern used for Frontiers save-and-resume and for NJDOL plain-language review.</p>
    </div>
    <p><button class="btn btn--secondary" type="button" id="ux-back" disabled>Back</button> <button class="btn" type="button" id="ux-next">Next step</button> <button class="btn" type="button" id="ux-save">Save draft</button></p>
</form>
<div id="ux-confirmation" class="alert alert--success" hidden><h2>Sample step complete</h2><p id="ux-confirmation-text"></p></div>

<h2>Interactive demo 2 — component states, documented the way a shared library documents them</h2>
<p>From the Frontiers shared component library: every component ships with its states and its accessibility notes, not just its default look.</p>
<table class="data-table">
    <caption>Component states and accessibility notes</caption>
